KG42.register("rules-129f69e0df9dd0655c4426c9",{"id":"09-local_flexibility::v141-c-R003","paper":"09-local_flexibility","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local market coordinator","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"The market operator forwards the service-request information without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay for each service. Scope: EcoGrid 2.0 auctions for DSO flexibility services. Condition: When forwarding the DSO's service-request information to aggregators. A DSO acting as market operator is incompatible with the unbundling principle. Scope: Market-operator role in the market-design framework discussed for EcoGrid 2.0. Condition: Under the unbundling principle separating network operation and commercial activities.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.local_operator under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"EcoGrid 2.0 auctions for DSO flexibility services. | Market-operator role in the market-design framework discussed for EcoGrid 2.0.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E11","v141-b-g01-E18"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The market operator forwards the service-request information without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay for each service. Scope: EcoGrid 2.0 auctions for DSO flexibility services. Condition: When forwarding the DSO's service-request information to aggregators.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E11","v141-b-g02-e06"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"A DSO acting as market operator is incompatible with the unbundling principle. Scope: Market-operator role in the market-design framework discussed for EcoGrid 2.0. Condition: Under the unbundling principle separating network operation and commercial activities.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E18"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-65c3762894-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3.2 Baseline flexibility services","anchor_text":"po IDs, Maximal price and a Activation probability. In addition, each service ﬂ is characterized thr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 4","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The process of how a service is requested, sold and activated is illustrated by a sequence diagram in Fig. 4. Initially, the DSO models the network load to identify when equipment might become overloaded. A list of potential flexibility service requests is created, which can mitigate the anticipated problem. The DSO initiates an auction by sending the list of service requests to the market operator. This happens one to twelve months ahead of time. The market operator forwards this information to the aggregators, without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay for each service. Aggregators then calculate at which cost they can provide the requested services and send offers for each service request on the list, which they want to provide."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-65c3762894-p03-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2. Literature review of local flexibility markets","anchor_text":"C. Heinrich, et al. power [35]. Therefore, network operation and commercial activities th have to be","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Therefore, network operation and commercial activities have to be strictly separated. This separation is called the unbundling principle. Accordingly, DSOs are not allowed to own power generation or consumption units [36]. Proposals in which DSOs can directly control DERs in order to mitigate congestions or manipulate voltages [37], or in which the DSO acts as a market operator [38], are not compatible with this principle."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-65c3762894-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3.2 Baseline flexibility services","anchor_text":"po IDs, Maximal price and a Activation probability. In addition, each service ﬂ is characterized thr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 4","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Flexibility services for the DSO target specific network components. This means that each service can only be delivered by specific DERs that are connected to specific nodes. Flexibility services proposed by EcoGrid 2.0 target grid nodes and lines which connect groups of customers, e.g. primary or secondary substations, as these are the parts in the network which might suffer the most from high load concurrency. The process of how a service is requested, sold and activated is illustrated by a sequence diagram in Fig. 4. Initially, the DSO models the network load to identify when equipment might become overloaded. A list of potential flexibility service requests is created, which can mitigate the anticipated problem. The DSO initiates an auction by sending the list of service requests to the market operator. This happens one to twelve months ahead of time. The market operator forwards this information to the aggregators, without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay for each service. Aggregators then calculate at which cost they can provide the requested services and send offers for each service request on the list, which they want to provide."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"The rule is a procedural coordination/regulatory rule. The supporting passages describe information forwarding between DSO, market operator, and aggregators, and an actor-role compatibility condition; they define no design-selection or operational decision variable with a domain.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed input or constant parameter is defined for C001/C002. The only numeric element in the supporting material for C001 is the lead-time description 'one to twelve months ahead of time' in E11, which is context for the auction process and is not part of the forwarding/no-reveal condition or of any formal expression."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E11","quote":"The market operator forwards this information to the aggregators, without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay for each service."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","quote":"Proposals in which DSOs can directly control DERs in order to mitigate congestions or manipulate voltages [37], or in which the DSO acts as a market operator [38], are not compatible with this principle."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"ffa45361d7e9643beeaef8d1a7ffca54304a5fbd5df4cf8f2c57a72a0d238930","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule contains two procedural/regulatory constraints: the market operator forwards DSO service-request information without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay, and a DSO acting as market operator is incompatible with the unbundling principle. The supplied candidate evidence describes process and actor-role conditions, not an equation, objective, optimization variable, or mathematical relation. No supported mathematical expression is present for these constraints. The B evidence remains unreviewed under the supplied experiment policy.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 constrains the coordination.local_operator function: the market operator forwards DSO service-request information without revealing the DSO's willingness to pay. The frozen graph has no edge for this rule, so the binding is to the only member node."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 constrains the same coordination.local_operator actor-role: a DSO acting as market operator is incompatible with the unbundling principle. The frozen graph has no edge for this rule."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
